 # Ambient Occlusion via Geometry/Neighbors
 
-Also called: Local Ambient Occlusion, Voxel AO, SDF-based AO
 
 ## Overview
-This family of methods estimates local ambient occlusion (AO) in volume rendering by querying geometric representations—such as Signed Distance Fields (SDFs), discrete voxel grids, or local density distributions—to approximate visibility without performing expensive global ray tracing against triangle meshes or screen-space depth buffers [S1, S2, S3, S4, 2527-ambient-occlusion-via-sdf-geometry.md, 6646-voxel-ambient-occlusion.md]. Unlike [JAOCC Shading](3634-jaocc-shading.md) which uses fixed cubic neighborhoods, or [Local Ambient Occlusion](4977-local-ambient-occlusion.md) which integrates transmittance along stochastic rays, these methods determine occlusion factors by directly evaluating geometric distance, neighbor occupancy, or gradient-weighted density, offering a computationally cheaper alternative suitable for real-time or interactive applications [G].
+This family of methods estimates local ambient occlusion in volume rendering by querying discrete voxel occupancy, geometric distances, or gradient-weighted density to approximate visibility, avoiding expensive global ray tracing or screen-space depth buffers [G]. Unlike [JAOCC Shading](3634-jaocc-shading.md) which uses fixed cubic neighborhoods, or [Local Ambient Occlusion](4977-local-ambient-occlusion.md) which integrates transmittance along stochastic rays, these techniques determine occlusion factors by directly evaluating geometric distance, neighbor occupancy, or gradient-weighted density, offering a computationally cheaper alternative suitable for real-time or interactive applications [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the core mechanism of evaluating visibility or illumination at a specific point $\mathbf{p}$ within or on the boundary of a volume, based on a light source direction $\mathbf{l}$ or environment [G].
-
-1.  **Visibility/Transmittance Evaluation:** The fundamental operation is determining how much light reaches $\mathbf{p}$ from a source [G]. This involves integrating the extinction coefficient $\sigma_t(\mathbf{x})$ along a path from the source to $\mathbf{p}$ (or vice versa) [G]. The transmittance $T$ is calculated as:
-    $$ T = \exp\left(-\int_{\mathbf{p}_{source}}^{\mathbf{p}} \sigma_t(\mathbf{x}) \, d\mathbf{x}\right) $$
-    In discrete implementations, this integral is approximated by summing $\sigma_t \cdot \Delta s$ over samples along the ray [G].
-
-2.  **Local Shading Models:** For surface-like appearance in volumes, normals are approximated from the volume gradient $\mathbf{g} = \nabla V$ [G]. Usually, a simple absorption and emission model is used because it is relatively cheap to evaluate and gives good enough results [S1].
-
-3.  **Shadowing Approximation:** Shadows are computed by testing occlusion along the direction to the light source [G].
-
-4.  **Ambient Occlusion:** Indirect illumination is approximated by integrating over the hemisphere or using simplified models (e.g., upward ray for sky light) [G]. The ambient contribution $L_{ambient}$ is often modulated by a shadowing factor based on local density:
-    $$ L_{ambient} = L_{sky} \cdot \exp(-D_{up}) \cdot AO_{factor} $$
-    where $D_{up}$ is the integrated density along an upward ray [G].
+Builds on [Local illumination, shadows and ambient occlusion](34-local-illumination-shadows-and-ambient-occlusion.md).
 
